"""Social and environmental impact assessment literacy."""


def build(course, module):
    return course(98, 'Social and Environmental Impact Assessment Literacy',
        'Social and\nEnvironmental Impact\nAssessment Literacy',
        'Interrogate a fictional extractive project’s impact assessment and write a traceable recommendation on evidence and mitigation.', [
        module('Define boundaries and baseline evidence',
            'Set defensible spatial, temporal and social boundaries for an impact question.',
            """Impact assessment asks what could change because of a proposed activity, how the change could occur, who might experience it and which alternatives could alter it. A baseline documents conditions before a specified decision or activity. Select an observation period and population that match the eventual comparison. The time before construction can already include exploration disruptions or anticipation of relocation. Describe those conditions rather than assuming the baseline represents a world without the project. [S85]

Boundaries should follow mechanisms. A noise or route change may affect people outside the area mapped for construction. A small change in distance can affect attendance differently for people who depend on public transport. A livelihood baseline should account for peak and lean periods, unpaid household production and multiple users of one site. Record which groups and seasonal activities the baseline misses. A sample from one easy-to-reach meeting venue can be valuable for exploring concerns, but its selection limits any estimate intended to describe the whole community. [S89]

Use multiple kinds of observation for different purposes. A service record can establish visits in a chosen period. An access diary can show a route or time barrier. A technical sampling report addresses a different question about environmental conditions. The UNEP Ogoniland assessment illustrates how an investigation can bring together distinct evidence streams and document its historical place and timeframe. Ask what each stream establishes before combining conclusions across them. [S90]

Document the version of each source, the question it informs and who is responsible for resolving a gap. When the missing evidence could reverse a decision or conceal a serious burden, keep that item open with a clear recheck date. Translate the missing item into a specific request so the authorised assessment team can respond.""",
            'A road assessment counts traffic near the gate during a dry-season weekday. Market users report disruption during wet-season trading.',
            'Specify a better boundary and historical period, then write two evidence requests that would test the reported disruption.',
            'Follow the route and market use across seasons. State the purpose and limitations of each observation and avoid generalising from one gate count.',
            """
Define baseline timing. | Construction started before the first traffic count. How should the count be described? | A documented early-construction baseline with that limitation. | An estimate of undisturbed pre-project traffic. | An excluded datum solely because it is recent. | A cause-of-delay measurement by itself. | Accurate timing determines which comparisons are possible.
Follow mechanisms. | A closure diverts journeys past a clinic outside the licence boundary. Where should assessment extend? | To the clinic route and its affected users. | To the gate only. | To every clinic in the country. | To project employees living inside the boundary. | The relevant boundary follows the effect being assessed.
Separate seasons. | Which comparison would best test disruption to seasonal market travel? | Comparable trading days in peak and lean conditions. | A peak market count and a single holiday. | An annual total drawn from one late arrival. | Several observations at the gate on the same quiet day. | The periods must represent the conditions named in the claim.
Choose fit evidence. | Which source addresses a claim about attendance at a clinic? | Comparable clinic visit records with stated inclusion rules. | A soil analysis alone. | An operator’s overall annual budget. | A list of companies using the site. | Select a source that actually measures the outcome.
Specify gaps. | An assessment has no record of tenant access. How should the gap be logged? | A defined tenant-access question, responsible owner and review date. | Zero tenant impacts. | Automatic cancellation of data already collected. | A pass supported by the landowner list. | The gap needs an accountable plan for checking.
"""),
        module('Assess pathways, significance and uncertainty',
            'Trace an impact pathway and explain how severity, likelihood and uncertainty affect the review.',
            """Draw out the steps between a project activity and a social outcome. A road change may increase travel time, which may reduce access to a market on trading mornings and affect earnings for specific vendors. Each step can be supported to a different degree. Name other factors that could explain a measured change, such as a cancelled market or school calendar. Ask whether feedback from the affected group supports the identified pathway and what additional observation could distinguish explanations. [S85]

Evaluate significance using clearly defined dimensions. A severe harm experienced by a small group deserves separate consideration. Describe duration, reversibility, how likely a change appears and whose capacity to respond may be limited. State how each label was assigned. Avoid inventing numerical likelihood when the evidence only supports a qualitative assessment. A matrix can help reviewers compare assumptions, but the values should lead back to evidence and affected people's accounts. Indicate clearly where a significant uncertainty or potentially irreversible loss changes what can be recommended. [S85]

Consider cumulative effects when two or more changes share receptors or pressure. A second closure on an alternative route can compound the difficulty of reaching a service even if each closure is locally reversible. Socio-economic conditions and past activities may affect who's already burdened. The Ghanaian mixed-methods study on perceived ecosystem losses shows why assessment should check how ordinary practices and cultural ties depend on the same resource. Treat its location and reported perceptions as limits on transfer. [R99]

Write one short review statement that captures pathway, affected groups, evidence grade and the unresolved decision. Keep implications proportionate. If the available evidence cannot identify a cause, explain what it does support. Specify what the responsible expert must verify and whose input needs to be heard before a consequential decision moves forward.""",
            'Most commuters retain access after a detour, but five street vendors lose the only bus that arrives before trade begins.',
            'Draw the effect chain, choose significance dimensions, and name a comparator and one potentially cumulative effect.',
            'Assess the early-morning vendor group separately. Consider duration, available alternatives, other closures and other possible explanations for lower earnings.',
            """
Trace a mechanism. | Which is the best representation of a detour’s potential effect on traders? | Detour changes arrival time, which may reduce viable selling hours. | Detour and lower sales prove every vendor lost income. | Lower sales establish a construction breach. | New route length shows the whole distribution of benefits. | The pathway identifies observable intermediate steps.
Disaggregate severity. | Most journeys succeed but one group loses its only service. How should impact be presented? | Report the concentrated loss separately alongside the overall result. | Average every journey into one category. | Weight the majority outcome as the single deciding result. | Remove unsuccessful cases until the route can be tested. | Serious impacts can be obscured in an aggregate.
Rate uncertainty. | Evidence supports a plausible mechanism but no estimate of frequency. What should the assessment state? | Describe the support and uncertainty in qualitative terms. | Assign a precise probability from the case narrative. | Treat unknown frequency as zero likelihood. | Present all outcomes as inevitable. | Use precision proportionate to evidence.
Identify cumulative pressure. | Another works project closes the route offered as an alternative. How should that be assessed? | Test overlapping restrictions for users who depend on both routes. | Treat route choices as independent regardless of timing. | Remove the second works project from the map. | Count the number of signs posted for each closure. | Shared routes and overlapping periods can create a combined effect.
Use perception studies. | What can a Ghanaian study of reported ecosystem loss help an assessor do in a new case? | Formulate local questions about lost uses and relationships. | Import the original household responses as new local evidence. | Determine land rights in the new site. | Diagnose the safety of a nearby supply. | Transfer the questions and verify local evidence separately.
"""),
        module('Test mitigation and follow-through',
            'Compare avoidance, minimisation, restoration and residual commitments with accountable measures.',
            """A credible response begins by comparing alternatives that avoid a material adverse impact. Once a project design is chosen, further changes can reduce some effects. Restoration addresses conditions or functions that have been damaged, and compensation may address eligible remaining loss where applicable. Keep choices visible in the written analysis. If a design was rejected, document the grounds and who reviewed it. Make explicit whether a proposed restoration is feasible and what evidence will show that it works in the relevant season. [S85]

Distinguish an action from a result. Building a footbridge is an action. People being able to reach a service safely and at an affordable cost when they need it is a possible result. Write both into a commitment. Define the affected population, the time window and any exclusion that changes interpretation. Decide how to handle missed observations without treating them as successes. Agree what would cause an action to be reconsidered and who can approve the adjustment. A clear outcome indicator makes it harder to substitute an easy process count for meaningful recovery. [S85]

An assessment summary intended for residents should explain the alternatives, foreseeable burdens and ways to question or correct the record in plain language. The EITI requirements make practical access to certain public assessments and monitoring documents a transparency concern where applicable. A disclosure can be available and still be difficult for people to use. Link the records to the specific action and date, avoid publishing private information, and confirm a route for people to ask about discrepancies. [S88]

When a plan depends on a technical finding or decision outside the social work role, record a condition rather than issuing clearance. In the review note, name the evidence expected, the owner and the consequence of failing the check. Set an interval for restarting the comparison if the population, timetable or feasible options change.""",
            'The designer proposes paying for a hardship fund without comparing a lower-impact route. Its proposed indicator counts the number of funded applications.',
            'Compare one avoidance option and one minimisation action. Write a measurable residual-outcome commitment and a trigger for checking it.',
            'Evaluate the lower-impact design first. Separate assistance awarded from restored access and establish who must review evidence if service use does not recover.',
            """
Compare avoidance early. | A redesigned route could keep a service accessible. When should the team compare it with a compensation proposal? | Before settling on a plan that assumes access loss. | After all payments are final. | When access monitoring is closed. | Only when both plans report equal spending. | Avoidance should be tested before relying on residual measures.
Measure results. | Which indicator shows whether a new crossing improved clinic access? | Share of the specified affected users able to reach appointments as needed. | Number of invoices signed for the bridge. | Visits to the operator’s project page. | Count of workers who built the crossing. | A result measures practical access for the population concerned.
Define a corrective trigger. | What belongs in a mitigation entry if a new route repeatedly fails for carers? | Named owner, failure threshold and time for adjusting the route. | An approval signature with no monitoring plan. | A target that counts every diverted trip as successful. | An operator statement about average performance only. | A trigger links observed failure to responsible action.
Make documents usable. | The relevant impact report is technically public but residents cannot find it. What would improve access? | Link the specific record in plain-language communication and explain its date and request route. | Give the file system location only to the operator. | Publish personal case records alongside it. | Report the programme name without any source. | Usability depends on people finding and understanding the relevant record.
Refer technical approval. | Who should authorise technical safety of a crossing in the fictional plan? | The qualified and empowered technical decision-maker. | The social worker who records user concerns. | The group with highest attendance. | The team that writes the press release. | The practitioner documents community access and refers technical decisions.
"""),
    ])
